<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<subsection xml:id="subsection-average-and-instantaneous-rates-of-change" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>
    Average and Instantaneous Rates of Change
  </title>

  <introduction>
    <p>
      The <term> average rate of change </term> of a real-valued function <m> f \colon X \to \R </m> defined on some subset <m> X \subseteq \R </m> which contains a closed interval <m> \clint{ a }{ b } \subseteq X </m> is the difference quotient
      
      <md>
        \frac{ f \of{ b } - f \of{ a } }{ b - a }
      </md>.
      
      In <xref ref="section-differentiability"/>, we saw that the derivative of a differentiable function is a limit of such difference quotients; as such, the derivative is often interpreted as an <term> instantaneous rate of change </term>. The <term> mean value theorem </term> relates these two rates of change under some mild hypotheses on <m> f </m>.
    </p>
  </introduction>
  
  <p>
    Before we explore the <xref ref="theorem-mean-value-theorem" text="title"/> in its full generality, we prove a special case. The proof of the <xref ref="theorem-mean-value-theorem" text="title"/> will rely on the following supporting lemma:
  </p>
  
  <lemma xml:id="lemma-rolles-lemma">
    <title>
      Rolle's lemma
    </title>
    
    <creator>
      Michel Rolle
    </creator>
    
    <statement>
      <p>
        Let <m> f \colon \clint{ a }{ b } \to \R </m> be a real-valued function defined on a closed interval <m> \clint{ a }{ b } \subsetneq \R </m>. If <m> f </m> is continuous on the closed interval <m> \clint{ a }{ b } </m> and differentiable on the open interval <m> \opint{ a }{ b } </m> and <m> f \of{ a } = f \of{ b } </m>, then <m> f' \of{ c } = 0 </m> for some point <m> a \lt c \lt b </m>.
      </p>
    </statement>
    
    <proof>
      <p>
        Let <m> a \lt c \lt b </m>, and suppose that <m> f' \of{ c } \gt 0 </m>. Then since <m> \frac{ f' \of{ c } }{ 2 } \gt 0 </m> is positive, there is some positive real number <m> \delta \gt 0 </m> so that
        
        <md>
          \abs{ \frac{ f \of{ x } - f \of{ c } }{ x - c } - f' \of{ c } } \lt \frac{ f' \of{ c } }{ 2 }
        </md>
        
        for all points <m> x \in \clint{ a }{ b } </m> so that <m> 0 \lt \abs{ x - c } \lt \delta </m>. In particular,
        
        <md>
          \frac{ f \of{ x } - f \of{ c } }{ x - c } \gt f' \of{ c } - \frac{ f' \of{ c } }{ 2 } = \frac{ f' \of{ c } }{ 2 } \gt 0
        </md>
        
        for all points <m> x \in \clint{ a }{ b } </m> so that <m> 0 \lt \abs{ x - c } \lt \delta </m>, so that <m> f \of{ x } - f \of{ c } </m> and <m> x - c </m> have the same sign; that is, so long as <m> x \in \clint{ a }{ b } </m> and <m> 0 \lt \abs{ x - c } \lt \delta </m>, then <m> f \of{ x } \lt f \of{ c } </m> if <m> x \lt c </m> and <m> f \of{ x } \gt f \of{ c } </m> if <m> x \gt c </m>. In particular, we conclude that <m> f </m> does not achieve a maximum or minimum at <m> c </m>.
      </p>
      
      <p>
        Similarly, now suppose that <m> f' \of{ c } \lt 0 </m>. Then since <m> -\frac{ f' \of{ c } }{ 2 } \gt 0 </m> is positive, there is some positive real number <m> \delta \gt 0 </m> so that
        
        <md>
          \abs{ \frac{ f \of{ x } - f \of{ c } }{ x - c } - f' \of{ c } } \lt -\frac{ f' \of{ c } }{ 2 }
        </md>
        
        for all points <m> x \in \clint{ a }{ b } </m> so that <m> 0 \lt \abs{ x - c } \lt \delta </m>. In particular,
        
        <md>
          \frac{ f \of{ x } - f \of{ c } }{ x - c } \lt -f' \of{ c } + \frac{ f' \of{ c } }{ 2 } = -\frac{ f' \of{ c } }{ 2 } \lt 0
        </md>
        
        for all points <m> x \in \clint{ a }{ b } </m> so that <m> 0 \lt \abs{ x - c } \lt \delta </m>, so that <m> f \of{ x } - f \of{ c } </m> and <m> x - c </m> have opposite sign; that is, so long as <m> x \in \clint{ a }{ b } </m> and <m> 0 \lt \abs{ x - c } \lt \delta </m>, then <m> f \of{ x } \gt f \of{ c } </m> if <m> x \lt c </m> and <m> f \of{ x } \lt f \of{ c } </m> if <m> x \gt c </m>. In particular, we conclude that <m> f </m> does not achieve a maximum or minimum at <m> c </m>.
      </p>
      
      <p>
        However, <m> \clint{ a }{ b } </m> is closed and bounded, and hence compact by <xref ref="corollary-compact-subsets-of-euclidean-space"/>. Since <m> f </m> is continuous and <m> \clint{ a }{ b } </m> is compact, <xref ref="proposition-continuous-images-of-compact-subsets"/> implies that
        
        <md>
          \im \of{ f } = f \of{ \clint{ a }{ b } } = \setmid{ f \of{ x } \in \R }{ a \leq x \leq b }
        </md>
        
        is compact and hence also closed and bounded. In particular, <m> f </m> attains a maximum <m> \max_{ a \leq x \leq b }{ f \of{ x } } = \max \of{ \im \of{ f } } </m> and a minimum <m> \min_{ a \leq x \leq b }{ f \of{ x } } = \min \of{ \im \of{ f } } </m>.
      </p>
      
      <p>
        To summarize our argument so far, we have shown that <m> f </m> does not achieve a maximum or minimum value at any point where its derivative is non-zero, but <m> f </m> must achieve a maximum and minimum value somewhere in <m> \clint{ a }{ b } </m>. If <m> f </m> achieves a maximum or a minimum value at an interior point <m> a \lt c \lt b </m>, then the above argument shows that <m> f' \of{ c } = 0 </m>. Otherwise, <m> f </m> attains both its maximum and minimum values at the endpoints <m> a </m> and <m> b </m>. In this case, <m> f </m> must be constant because <m> f \of{ a } = f \of{ b } </m>. In particular, this implies that <m> f' \of{ c } = 0 </m> for all points <m> a \lt c \lt b </m>.
      </p>
    </proof>
  </lemma>
  
  <p>
    We remark that the details of the above proof of <xref ref="lemma-rolles-lemma" text="title"/> are necessary even if we have an understanding of the analysis of critical points and local extrema. Indeed, the result often taught in calculus classes that for a function <m> f \colon X \to \R </m>, if <m> f </m> is differentiable at a point <m> c \in X </m> and <m> f' \of{ c } \gt 0 </m>, then <m> f </m> is increasing near <m> c </m> is false; such a conclusion actually requires continuous differentiability near <m> c </m>.
  </p>
  
  <theorem xml:id="theorem-mean-value-theorem">
    <title>
      Mean value theorem
    </title>
    
    <creator>
      Joseph-Louis Lagrange
    </creator>
    
    <statement>
      <p>
        Let <m> f \colon \clint{ a }{ b } \to \R </m> be a real-valued function defined on a closed interval <m> \clint{ a }{ b } \subsetneq \R </m>. If <m> f </m> is continuous on the closed interval <m> \clint{ a }{ b } </m> and differentiable on the open interval <m> \opint{ a }{ b } </m>, then
        
        <md>
          f' \of{ c } = \frac{ f \of{ b } - f \of{ a } }{ b - a }
        </md>
        
        for some point <m> a \lt c \lt b </m>.
      </p>
    </statement>
    
    <proof>
      <p>
        Let <m> g \colon \clint{ a }{ b } \to \R </m> be the real-valued function defined by the formula
        
        <md>
          g \of{ x } = f \of{ x } - \of{ \frac{ f \of{ b } - f \of{ a } }{ b - a } } x
        </md>.
        
        Then <m> g </m> is continuous by <xref ref="proposition-vector-arithmetic-of-continuous-maps"/> and differentiable on the open interval <m> \opint{ a }{ b } </m> by <xref ref="proposition-arithmetic-of-differentiable-functions"/>, with derivative
        
        <md>
          g' \of{ x } = f' \of{ x } - \frac{ f \of{ b } - f \of{ a } }{ b - a }
        </md>.
        
        Moreover,
        
        <md>
          <mrow>
            g \of{ b } \amp = f \of{ b } - \of{ \frac{ f \of{ b } - f \of{ a } }{ b - a } } b
          </mrow>
          
          <mrow>
            \amp = f \of{ b } - \of{ \frac{ f \of{ b } - f \of{ a } }{ b - a } } \of{ b - a } - \of{ \frac{ f \of{ b } - f \of{ a } }{ b - a } } a
          </mrow>
          
          <mrow>
            \amp = f \of{ b } - \of{ f \of{ b } - f \of{ a } } - \of{ \frac{ f \of{ b } - f \of{ a } }{ b - a } } a
          </mrow>
          
          <mrow>
            \amp = f \of{ b } - \of{ \frac{ f \of{ b } - f \of{ a } }{ b - a } } a = g \of{ a }
          </mrow>
        </md>.
        
        <xref ref="lemma-rolles-lemma"/> now implies that <m> g' \of{ c } = 0 </m> for some interior point <m> a \lt c \lt b </m>; that is,
        
        <md>
          f' \of{ c } = \frac{ f \of{ b } - f \of{ a } }{ b - a }
        </md>.
      </p>
    </proof>
  </theorem>

  <example xml:id="example-hypotheses-of-the-mean-value-theorem">
    <title>
      Hypotheses of the <xref ref="theorem-mean-value-theorem" text="title"/>
    </title>
    
    <statement>
      <p>
        The following examples demonstrate that each of the hypotheses of the <xref ref="theorem-mean-value-theorem" text="title"/> is necessary:
        
        <ol>
          <li>
            <p>
              Let <m> f \colon \clint{ 0 }{ 1 } \cup \clint{ 2 }{ 3 } \to \R </m> be the real-valued function defined piecewise by the formula
              
              <md>
                f \of{ x } =
                  \begin{cases}
                    0 \amp 0 \leq x \leq 1 \\
                    1 \amp 2 \leq x \leq 3
                  \end{cases}
              </md>.
              
              Then <m> f </m> is continuous on <m> \clint{ 0 }{ 1 } \cup \clint{ 2 }{ 3 } </m> and differentiable on the interior <m> \opint{ 0 }{ 1 } \cup \opint{ 2 }{ 3 } </m>, but
              
              <md>
                f' \of{ x } = 0 \neq \frac{ 1 }{ 3 } = \frac{ 1 - 0 }{ 3 - 0 } = \frac{ f \of{ 3 } - f \of{ 0 } }{ 3 - 0 }
              </md>
              
              for all interior points <m> x \in \opint{ 0 }{ 1 } \cup \opint{ 2 }{ 3 } </m>. This example shows that <m> f </m> must be defined on a closed interval in order to guarantee the conclusions of the <xref ref="theorem-mean-value-theorem" text="title"/>.
            </p>
          </li>
          
          <li>
            <p>
              Let <m> f \colon \clint{ 0 }{ 1 } \to \R </m> be the real-valued function defined piecewise by the formula
              
              <md>
                f \of{ x } =
                  \begin{cases}
                    0 \amp 0 \leq x \lt 1 \\
                    1 \amp x = 1
                  \end{cases}
              </md>.
              
              Then <m> f </m> is continuous on <m> \clopint{ 0 }{ 1 } </m> and differentiable on the interior <m> \opint{ 0 }{ 1 } </m>, but
              
              <md>
                f' \of{ x } = 0 \neq 1 = \frac{ 1 - 0 }{ 1 - 0 } = \frac{ f \of{ 1 } - f \of{ 0 } }{ 1 - 0 }
              </md>
              
              for all interior points <m> x \in \opint{ 0 }{ 1 } </m>. This example shows that <m> f </m> must be continuous on the closed interval <m> \clint{ 0 }{ 1 } </m> in order to guarantee the conclusions of the <xref ref="theorem-mean-value-theorem" text="title"/>.
            </p>
          </li>
          
          <li>
            <p>
              Let <m> f \colon \clint{ -1 }{ 1 } \to \R </m> be the real-valued function defined by the formula <m> f \of{ x } = \abs{ x } </m>. Then <m> f </m> is continuous on <m> \clint{ 0 }{ 1 } </m> and differentiable at every interior point except <m> x = 0 </m>, but
              
              <md>
                f' \of{ x } = \pm 1 \neq 0 = \frac{ 1 - 1 }{ 1 - \of{ -1 } } = \frac{ f \of{ 1 } - f \of{ -1 } }{ 1 - \of{ -1 } }
              </md>
              
              for all interior points <m> x \in \opint{ -1 }{ 1 } \setminus \set{ 0 } </m>. This example shows that <m> f </m> must be differentiable on the open interval <m> \opint{ -1 }{ 1 } </m> in order to guarantee the conclusions of the <xref ref="theorem-mean-value-theorem" text="title"/>.
            </p>
          </li>
        </ol>
      </p>
    </statement>
  </example>

  <conclusion>
    <p>
      While all of the hypotheses of the <xref ref="theorem-mean-value-theorem" text="title"/> are necessary in order to guarantee its conclusion, they are sufficient as written. Indeed, a function <m> f \colon \clint{ a }{ b } \to \R </m> need not be differentiable at the endpoints <m> a </m> or <m> b </m> in order to apply the <xref ref="theorem-mean-value-theorem" text="title"/>; for an example, see the function defined by the formula
      
      <md>
        f \of{ x } = \sqrt{ \of{ \frac{ a - b }{ 2 } }^{ 2 } - \of{ x - \frac{ a + b }{ 2 } }^{ 2 } }
      </md>,
      
      which is differentiable on the open interval <m> \opint{ a }{ b } </m> but not at the endpoints and yet satisfies the conclusions of the <xref ref="theorem-mean-value-theorem" text="title"/>.
    </p>
  </conclusion>
</subsection>
